# SESSION 2026-08-08 (part 2) â€” publicmatch failure + native reader fully mapped

## Finding 1 (PROVEN) â€” the engine DOES honour the type-0 terminator

This CORRECTS the 2026-08-07 entry "Root cause: the terminator is ignored, so the
reader steps onto the footer and reads 0x1D". That described MY offline reader's
`--simulate ignore-terminator` mode. It is NOT what the engine does.

`CL_Demo_ReadDemoMessage` @ 0x9188C0, at LABEL_42:

    sub_AF9B70(&v39, 1, client, clc);        // read 1 byte through clc+346144
    if (v39) { switch (v39) { 1,2,3,4; default: Com_Error(1,"EXE_ERR_PROCESS_DEMO_FILE_FAILED"); } }
    else     { /* clean EOF: queues "disconnect", sets completed, returns 0 */ }

`if (v39)` IS the terminator check. Do not "fix" the terminator handling; it is
already correct. `v39` lives in the arg0 home slot and is only written on a
successful read, so a failed read leaves STALE data there.

## Finding 2 (PROVEN) â€” reads succeeded, so publicmatch is a genuine desync

`CL_Demo_Read` @ 0x912260 raises `EXE_ERR_READING_DEMO` on any short read.
That error did NOT appear in the log. Therefore the 1-byte read SUCCEEDED and
returned a value that was neither 0 nor 1..4.

## Finding 3 (PROVEN) â€” the .demo header, straight from CL_Demo_Play_f

Read into `clc + 482656*client + 262760` by `sub_9192E0(client)` (still unnamed).

    +0x00  version    must == 29        else EXE_ERR_INVALID_DEMO_FILE
    +0x04  headerSize must == 79384     else EXE_ERR_INVALID_DEMO_FILE
    +0x08  dword  -> Dvar_SetInt(cl_demo_client, v)     recording client slot
    +0x0D  byte   -> sub_1973F0(sub_197040(), b)  == *(DWORD*)(x+40) = b   (plain setter)
    +0x18  dword  must == 5             else Com_Error(1,"428 %s %s")
    +0x1C  0x135FC bytes memcpy'd to PlaybackData+76   (0x1C + 0x135FC == 79384)

Shipped-demo header diff (4 working demos are byte-identical almost everywhere):

              +0x08 +0x0C   +0x10       +0x14      +0x48    +0x4C +0x54
    working     0    256   2180834731  3108103370  0xA0000    0     0
    publicmatch 7      0    122260590    17825793  0x80000    1     2

`+0x0C == 256` matches the archive ring size proven in Finding 4. publicmatch has 0.
NOT the failure cause though: publicmatch cleared every validated field (no
EXE_ERR_INVALID_DEMO_FILE and no "428"), so the header was accepted.

## Finding 4 (PROVEN) â€” archive packet == 185 bytes, my model matches the engine

`CL_Demo_ProcessPacket_Type1` @ 0x918640:
  read idx(4) -> entry = clientActive + 188*idx + 42344
  6 reads: 12,12,4,4,4,12
  1 read: gate(4) into entry+48
  if (entry+48 != 0) 2 more reads (4 then 2)   else zero entry+52 / entry+56
  1 read: 128-byte tail
  ring wraps via `(idx+1) & 0x800000FF`  -> 256 ENTRIES, 188 bytes each

1 + 4 + 48 + 4 + 128 == 185. Verified: the gate is 0 in 100% of packets across ALL
FIVE shipped demos (measured with the conditional both honoured and ignored â€” both
walks are byte-identical and both reach a clean terminator). The conditional +6 has
never once fired. The 185-byte model is CORRECT.

## Finding 5 (PROVEN) â€” snapshot packet, and what "430" means

`CL_Demo_ProcessPacket_Type2_3` @ 0x919510, a2 = (type == 3):
  alloc 0x20000 scratch; read seq(4) -> clc dword 32847
  if (type 3) read extra(4) -> clc dword 32850
  MSG_InitWrite(msg, buf, 0x20000); read len(4)
  if (len > msg.maxsize) Com_Error(1,"430")      <-- "430" == snapshot exceeds 128 KB
  read len bytes; MSG_ReadLong -> messageSequence (& 0x7FFFFFFF)
  if (newSeq >= lastSeq - 128) CL_ParseServerMessage(...)   else DROP (bytes still consumed)

publicmatch's largest packet is 5743 bytes, far under 0x20000, and "430" never fired.
So the snapshot path is NOT the desync either.

## Finding 6 (PROVEN) â€” CL_Demo_ReadFooter restores the cursor only on success

    v2 = PlaybackData+24                      // save cursor (79384 after the header)
    seek(-8, SEEK_END); read magic(4), size(4)
    if (magic != expected) return 0           // <-- cursor LEFT IN THE FOOTER
    seek(-8 - size, SEEK_END); read tables
    caps: one count must be <= 40, another <= 300, a tag must == 3984
    if (CL_Demo_ReadNetConstStringTable(...)) { seek(v2, SEEK_SET); PlaybackData+24 = v2; return 1 }
    return 0                                  // <-- cursor LEFT IN THE FOOTER

Every failure path leaks the file position into the footer. Harmless today only
because Play_f turns a 0 return into EXE_ERR_INVALID_DEMO_FILE and aborts. That error
did not fire for publicmatch, so its cursor WAS correctly restored to 79384.

## Finding 7 (PROVEN) â€” there is a 2 MB in-memory demo buffer with a second reader

The read callback at `clc + 482656*client + 346144` is swapped between two readers:
  - `CL_Demo_Read`  @ 0x912260 â€” reads the FILE, accumulates PlaybackData+24
  - `sub_919190`               â€” memcpy from `PlaybackData + 3336316 + cursor`

PlaybackData map (all offsets from qword_10F340A0, which CL_Demo_GetPlaybackData returns):
    +24       bytes consumed from the FILE   <-- the money field
    +3336304  mode: 3 = playing, 2 = ended (set by sub_91A0B0), 1 = clip capture
    +3336316  start of a 2 MB buffer         (5433468 - 3336316 == 2097152 exactly)
    +5433468  buffer fill level; ReadDemoMessage bails if mode==1 && fill >= 0x1F0000
    +5439380  block descriptor table, stride 65704, fields +0 offset / +4 length
    +6096400  block count   +6096404  block index   +6096408  buffer read cursor
    +6096396 / +6096413  two mode flags

## Finding 8 (PROVEN) â€” publicmatch is structurally unlike every working demo

                type2   type1   ratio  p99 len  max len   client  map
    airship      1735   19869   1:11      82      1175      0     mp_airship
    egyptbots    2066   14654   1:7      429      2169      0     mp_egypt_02
    gibraltar    1958   21404   1:11      74      3242      0     mp_gibraltar_02
    dday         1175   12731   1:11     105      5089      0     mp_d_day
    publicmatch  9244    8945   1:1     1244      5743      7     mp_d_day

But it PARSES CLEANLY: all 18189 packets walk to a valid terminator at 0x2D1EC4
under the engine's exact rules. THE FILE IS NOT CORRUPT.

## Finding 9 (PROVEN) â€” demo_game::clc_for() reads the WRONG GLOBAL

`mov rbp, cs:clc` @ 0x9108FA is a LOAD, so `clc` is a POINTER VARIABLE living at
IDA 0x1BD3D00 (literal 0x1BD2D00). Confirmed by xref count:

    IDA 0x1BD3D00  100+ xrefs: CL_ParseSnapshot, CL_ParseGamestate, CL_Disconnect,
                   CL_WritePacket, CL_FirstSnapshot, every CL_Demo_*   <-- the real clc
    IDA 0x1BD3C00  3 xrefs, none client code (sub_303D0/sub_E7980/sub_3D6030)

`demo_game.hpp:478` uses `0x1BD2C00_b` == IDA 0x1BD3C00. It is 0x100 too low and
points at an unrelated variable. Its own comment says 0x1BD3D00, so the comment is
right and the literal is wrong. Affects clc_server_message_sequence,
clc_reliable_acknowledge, clc_client_reliable_acknowledge,
clc_client_reliable_sequence â€” i.e. reads AND WRITES through a bogus base.

NOT YET FIXED â€” it is in working custom-demo code and changing it alters live
behaviour, so it needs a deliberate decision, not a drive-by edit.

## Open question, and the probe built to answer it

The file is clean and every packet handler matches the engine, yet the engine
desynced. Remaining candidates:
  (a) runaway pump â€” the feed loop burned to EOF during level load, ran past the
      terminator into the footer and read 0x1D (== 29, the version dword). This
      matches the user's recollection of the historic failure exactly.
  (b) wrong read callback â€” clc+346144 still pointing at sub_919190 over an
      unfilled 2 MB buffer, or a stale v39 from a call that never wrote it.

These are separated by ONE number: PlaybackData+24 at the moment of the error.
  ~0x2D1EC5 => (a).   small => (b), and it names the exact desyncing packet.

PROBE ONLY, built and deployed 2026-08-08, additive (src/demo/demo_native.cpp only):
  - `dump_playback_state()` prints FILEPOS, mode, membuf fill/cursor/block, and the
    live read-callback address resolved to IDA form.
  - fires automatically from the Com_Error hook when the caller is in CL_Demo_*
    (IDA 0x90F000..0x920000).
  - `demo_native_state` console command dumps the same on demand during playback.


